\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces The FD10D's quantum efficiency depends on its reverse bias; the increase in quantum efficiency was at the $\sim 15\%$ level when the bias was increased to its maximum datasheet value. Two types of measurement were used to determine the increase in quantum efficiency as the bias was increased. The first was to measure the DC response and the corresponding results are shown as circles. The second method involved modulating the bias and measuring the resulting modulation in the photocurrent, i.e.\nobreakspace  {}an AC measurement, and these are shown by the stars. Each set of measurements was done with a different intensity by changing either the beam's width or power.\relax }}{19}{figure.caption.30}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10dqeasfnofbias}{{1.2}{19}{The FD10D's quantum efficiency depends on its reverse bias; the increase in quantum efficiency was at the $\sim 15\%$ level when the bias was increased to its maximum datasheet value. Two types of measurement were used to determine the increase in quantum efficiency as the bias was increased. The first was to measure the DC response and the corresponding results are shown as circles. The second method involved modulating the bias and measuring the resulting modulation in the photocurrent, i.e.~an AC measurement, and these are shown by the stars. Each set of measurements was done with a different intensity by changing either the beam's width or power.\relax }{figure.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{20}{section*.32}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces The bias of the FD10D was set to 0.6\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V and it was exposed to light\leavevmode {\color {red}\ref  {}find values}. The noise of the photocurrent produced by the FD10D (blue line) corresponds to the Equation \ref  {eq:pd_chapter_noise_budget} (orange line) meaning that that the quantum efficiency of the FD10D did increase rather than the increase in photocurrent being the result of an avalanche process. The in-loop signal (red line) was used to cross-check the calibration of the measurement. \relax }}{21}{figure.caption.33}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10dnoisetest}{{1.3}{21}{The bias of the FD10D was set to 0.6\,V and it was exposed to light\textcolor {red}{\ref {}find values}. The noise of the photocurrent produced by the FD10D (blue line) corresponds to the Equation \ref {eq:pd_chapter_noise_budget} (orange line) meaning that that the quantum efficiency of the FD10D did increase rather than the increase in photocurrent being the result of an avalanche process. The in-loop signal (red line) was used to cross-check the calibration of the measurement. \relax }{figure.caption.33}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces The bias of the FD10D was set to 0.6\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V and it was exposed to a series of different power levels to check at what photocurrent the diode would saturate at. This was done by measuring the height of the calibration peak; if this peak decreased in size relative to the DC signal then this would indicate the AC signal was saturating. This peak could only be measured to within 1\%, and so the FD10D showed no sign of saturation under these conditions.\relax }}{22}{figure.caption.34}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10dacdc}{{1.4}{22}{The bias of the FD10D was set to 0.6\,V and it was exposed to a series of different power levels to check at what photocurrent the diode would saturate at. This was done by measuring the height of the calibration peak; if this peak decreased in size relative to the DC signal then this would indicate the AC signal was saturating. This peak could only be measured to within 1\%, and so the FD10D showed no sign of saturation under these conditions.\relax }{figure.caption.34}{}}
