\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces The dark noise of the FD10D increases as the reverse bias is increased. Above 0.6\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V, the dark noise of the FD10D would limit the noise of a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current. As the noise spectral densities have no knees, the function $i_t/f^{\beta }$ can be fitted to the it. See appendix \ref  {sec:pd_appendix_bias_dark_noise} for the dark noise spectra of other photodiodes. Below 0.9\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V reverse bias, the spectra fall as $f^{-0.45}$ which is typical for `1/f' noise. Above 0.9\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V bias the spectra fall as $f^{-0.4}$. This data is shown along side the results for the other diodes tested in Figure \ref  {fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot}.\relax }}{25}{figure.caption.39}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10ddarknoise}{{1.5}{25}{The dark noise of the FD10D increases as the reverse bias is increased. Above 0.6\,V, the dark noise of the FD10D would limit the noise of a 10\,mA current. As the noise spectral densities have no knees, the function $i_t/f^{\beta }$ can be fitted to the it. See appendix \ref {sec:pd_appendix_bias_dark_noise} for the dark noise spectra of other photodiodes. Below 0.9\,V reverse bias, the spectra fall as $f^{-0.45}$ which is typical for `1/f' noise. Above 0.9\,V bias the spectra fall as $f^{-0.4}$. This data is shown along side the results for the other diodes tested in Figure \ref {fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot}.\relax }{figure.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Left panel: the dark current for each diode was measured. Diodes of a similar cut-off wavelength share the same marker shape. The 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm photodiodes have larger markers than the 0.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm photodiodes. Right panel: the dark noise at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz for each diode. The dashed line indicates the level of shot noise for a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current.\relax }}{26}{figure.caption.40}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot}{{1.6}{26}{Left panel: the dark current for each diode was measured. Diodes of a similar cut-off wavelength share the same marker shape. The 1\,mm photodiodes have larger markers than the 0.5\,mm photodiodes. Right panel: the dark noise at 10\,Hz for each diode. The dashed line indicates the level of shot noise for a 10\,mA current.\relax }{figure.caption.40}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.7}{\ignorespaces The difference in dark noise between two Hamamatsu photodiodes (g12182-010k) of the same kind under identical operating conditions (room temperature at 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V bias). Diode 2 performs nearly 100 times better than diode 1. \relax }}{27}{figure.caption.41}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterg12182-010knoisecompare}{{1.7}{27}{The difference in dark noise between two Hamamatsu photodiodes (g12182-010k) of the same kind under identical operating conditions (room temperature at 1\,V bias). Diode 2 performs nearly 100 times better than diode 1. \relax }{figure.caption.41}{}}
