\newlabel{fig:pdchaptercoolingapparatusphotos}{{1.8}{29}{(a) The photodiode was affixed to this aluminium block. The power resistors provided heating to the block and the copper rod provided a thermal link between the liquid nitrogen dewar and the block. (b) A photodiode glued to the block such that a large proportion of the photodiode was in contact with it.\relax }{figure.caption.44}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1.8}Excess Noise in the IG24x500S4i}{29}{section.1.8}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{29}{section*.47}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.9}{\ignorespaces Left panel: the dark current of each photodiode at their maximum datasheet specified reverse bias as a function of temperature. The apparatus limited dark current measurements to a couple of nanoamps. Exponentials were fitted to the data. Diodes with similar cut-off wavelengths share marker shapes, and 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm diodes have larger markers than the 0.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm ones. Right panel: the dark noise of each diode was measured as a function of temperature. The black dashed line indicates the shot noise level for a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current. At 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the measurements for the IG24x500S4i did not change much as the temperature changed, there was a knee around this frequency (see Figure \ref  {fig:pdappendixig24temperaturenoisecurrent}); the noise of the IG24x500S4i was $600-700\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\pico \ampere /\sqrt  {\hertz }}$ at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz.\relax }}{30}{figure.caption.45}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterdarkcurrenttermperaturealldiodes}{{1.9}{30}{Left panel: the dark current of each photodiode at their maximum datasheet specified reverse bias as a function of temperature. The apparatus limited dark current measurements to a couple of nanoamps. Exponentials were fitted to the data. Diodes with similar cut-off wavelengths share marker shapes, and 1\,mm diodes have larger markers than the 0.5\,mm ones. Right panel: the dark noise of each diode was measured as a function of temperature. The black dashed line indicates the shot noise level for a 10\,mA current. At 10\,Hz, the measurements for the IG24x500S4i did not change much as the temperature changed, there was a knee around this frequency (see Figure \ref {fig:pdappendixig24temperaturenoisecurrent}); the noise of the IG24x500S4i was $600-700\,\si {\pico \ampere /\sqrt {\hertz }}$ at 10\,Hz.\relax }{figure.caption.45}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.10}{\ignorespaces The saturation limit of three Laser Components photodiodes was measured. The photodiodes were biased so that their noise at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz was equal to the shot noise of a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current. The IG24x500S4i and IG26x500S4i were exposed to a beam whose $3\sigma $ width was 220\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \metre } and the IG22x1000S4i was exposed to a beam whose $3\sigma $ width was 1000\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \metre }. The size of the a light modulation whose relative size was known was tracked as the DC photocurrent was increased; the more saturated a photodiode is, the smaller the amount of signal was detected. These diodes had low saturations limits compared to the FD10D, which showed no signs of saturation up to 16.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA (see Figure \ref  {fig:PD_chapter_fd10d_AC_DC}).\relax }}{31}{figure.caption.46}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterlasercomponentsratio}{{1.10}{31}{The saturation limit of three Laser Components photodiodes was measured. The photodiodes were biased so that their noise at 10\,Hz was equal to the shot noise of a 10\,mA current. The IG24x500S4i and IG26x500S4i were exposed to a beam whose $3\sigma $ width was 220\,\si {\micro \metre } and the IG22x1000S4i was exposed to a beam whose $3\sigma $ width was 1000\,\si {\micro \metre }. The size of the a light modulation whose relative size was known was tracked as the DC photocurrent was increased; the more saturated a photodiode is, the smaller the amount of signal was detected. These diodes had low saturations limits compared to the FD10D, which showed no signs of saturation up to 16.5\,mA (see Figure \ref {fig:PD_chapter_fd10d_AC_DC}).\relax }{figure.caption.46}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.11}{\ignorespaces The noise of a 1.3\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA of photocurrent made by the IG24x500S4i (blue) exceeded the expected amount (red) based on Equation \ref  {eq:pd_chapter_noise_budget} by a factor of $1.75$. This beam was not saturating the diode as the height of the 1111\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz signal was the the right level. This excess noise was also found at 0.25\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA of photocurrent.\relax }}{32}{figure.caption.48}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchaptershotnoiseproblemig24}{{1.11}{32}{The noise of a 1.3\,mA of photocurrent made by the IG24x500S4i (blue) exceeded the expected amount (red) based on Equation \ref {eq:pd_chapter_noise_budget} by a factor of $1.75$. This beam was not saturating the diode as the height of the 1111\,Hz signal was the the right level. This excess noise was also found at 0.25\,mA of photocurrent.\relax }{figure.caption.48}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{32}{section*.50}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.12}{\ignorespaces A noise projection measurement was performed to determine if the excess noise in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i (see Figure \ref  {fig:pdchaptershotnoiseproblemig24}) was due to bias noise. The left hand panels show the bode plot of the transfer function between the photodiode's cathode and the output of the transmipedance amplifier when the photodiode was producing 1.3\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA of photocurrent. The right hand panel shows the effect of subtracting bias noise from the signal at the output of the transimpedance amplifier. The peak at 1121\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz was a modulation applied to the photodiode's cathode; 42.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}dB of this signal was removed by using the noise projection technique which is in close agreement with the amount expected from the measured transfer function. However, the level of the broadband noise remained unchanged thus indicating that the excess noise witnessed in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i was not due to bias noise.\relax }}{33}{figure.caption.49}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapternoiseprojection}{{1.12}{33}{A noise projection measurement was performed to determine if the excess noise in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i (see Figure \ref {fig:pdchaptershotnoiseproblemig24}) was due to bias noise. The left hand panels show the bode plot of the transfer function between the photodiode's cathode and the output of the transmipedance amplifier when the photodiode was producing 1.3\,mA of photocurrent. The right hand panel shows the effect of subtracting bias noise from the signal at the output of the transimpedance amplifier. The peak at 1121\,Hz was a modulation applied to the photodiode's cathode; 42.5\,dB of this signal was removed by using the noise projection technique which is in close agreement with the amount expected from the measured transfer function. However, the level of the broadband noise remained unchanged thus indicating that the excess noise witnessed in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i was not due to bias noise.\relax }{figure.caption.49}{}}
\citation{Beattie1962}
\newlabel{eq:pd_chapter_ideality_factor}{{1.4}{34}{}{equation.1.8.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{34}{section*.53}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1.9}Properties of an InSb detector}{34}{section.1.9}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.13}{\ignorespaces To investigate possible reasons why there was excess noise in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i, its ideality factor was measured (red points). Equation \ref  {eq:pd_chapter_ideality_factor} was used to do an unweighed fit to the data ($I_0 = (360\pm 10)\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {nA}$ and $n = 1.02\pm 0.01$). The IG24x500S4i was found to be close to ideal, as $n$ was close to 1, and so this does not provide clues to the source of excess noise in the photocurrent.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.51}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterforwardbiasiv}{{1.13}{35}{To investigate possible reasons why there was excess noise in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i, its ideality factor was measured (red points). Equation \ref {eq:pd_chapter_ideality_factor} was used to do an unweighed fit to the data ($I_0 = (360\pm 10)\,\si {nA}$ and $n = 1.02\pm 0.01$). The IG24x500S4i was found to be close to ideal, as $n$ was close to 1, and so this does not provide clues to the source of excess noise in the photocurrent.\relax }{figure.caption.51}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.14}{\ignorespaces Different levels light levels were shone onto the photodiode and the current produced at 0.05\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V reverse bias was recorded. These measurements are shown by the stars. Based on the model obtained from the data shown in Figure \ref  {fig:pdchapterforwardbiasiv}, the current flowing through the diode was predicted (indicated by the lines). The model and the measurements are in agreement with each other although the diode may have been saturating for the 3.3\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mW measurement.\relax }}{36}{figure.caption.52}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterforwardbiasmodelwithlight}{{1.14}{36}{Different levels light levels were shone onto the photodiode and the current produced at 0.05\,V reverse bias was recorded. These measurements are shown by the stars. Based on the model obtained from the data shown in Figure \ref {fig:pdchapterforwardbiasiv}, the current flowing through the diode was predicted (indicated by the lines). The model and the measurements are in agreement with each other although the diode may have been saturating for the 3.3\,mW measurement.\relax }{figure.caption.52}{}}
\citation{Kimukin2003}
